            <ArticleTitle>Dehiscence of the infraorbital canal with the maxillary antral empyema: a new cause of facial pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e227-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/SCS.0000000000001479</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The infraorbital nerve is the largest cutaneous branch of the maxillary divisions of the trigeminal nerve. It may produce a bony ridge on the antral roof but usually goes through within the maxillary bone as a discrete canal. Rarely, it could be partially or completely dehiscent, lying submucosally on the antral roof as in this case. We describe a case of longstanding facial pain because of dehiscence of the infraorbital canal associated with the maxillary antral empyema. Endoscopic sinus surgery was successful in relieving the symptom.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
